---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Explorer from '../../components/snp1/Explorer.tsx';
---

<Base
  title="SNP1 strategy"
  description="Hold the single largest US company by market value and check the ranking once a month. Backtested from 2000 against SPY."
>
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <h1>SNP1 strategy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Hold the single largest US company by market value. Check the ranking on the 1st of each month, switch only if #1
      has changed, and add new money monthly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap">
    <Explorer client:only="react">
      <p slot="fallback" style="margin-top:2rem">Loading the backtest…</p>
    </Explorer>

    <section class="context">
      <h2>How to read this</h2>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>
          <strong>This is a concentrated bet that works in some decades and fails in others.</strong> Michael Mauboussin
          and Dan Callahan at Counterpoint Global studied US stocks from 1950 to 2023 using CRSP data. They report that
          owning the largest stock trailed the S&amp;P 500 by about 4.3 percentage points a year over the full period,
          but beat it by about 14 points a year from 2014 to 2023. The years 2000 to 2013 were poor: the largest
          companies then were Microsoft, General Electric and Cisco.
        </p>
        <p>
          The largest company is usually there because its price has risen a lot. Holding it is a bet that the rise
          continues. Treat a good recent record as a feature of the period, not proof of the rule.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          Figures in this box are as reported in “Stock Market Concentration” (Counterpoint Global, Morgan Stanley
          Investment Management, 2024). They are not recomputed here.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Survivorship bias</h2>
      <div class="panel">
        <p>
          The ranking table was researched after the fact, from sources that are easier to find for companies that are
          still large today. Prices come from a source that drops delisted companies. Both make the past look tidier
          than it was to live through. No company that reached #1 since 2000 has been delisted, so the SNP1 line is less
          affected than the top-7 variants, where rows marked unsure matter more.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .context {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.9rem;
  }
  .context h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }
  .context .panel p {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
</style>
